[Music]  in a major moment for the modern voting  rights fight house democrats unveiled a  long awaited john lewis voting rights  bill aimed at restoring the voting  rights act of 1965. alabama  representative terry sewell announced  the bill in front of the historic edmund  petters bridge the site where during the  1965 march to montgomery selma police  brutally beat lewis and martin luther  king jr and his fellow marchers in what  became known as bloody sunday  as many of you know  the voting rights act of 1965  was only made possible because of the  personal sacrifices of amazing foot  soldiers many known and unknown  right here on this bridge in my hometown  56 years ago on bloody sunday as john  lewis would say  he shed a little blood on this bridge  for the equal right to vote for all  americans  the bill to protect and restore voting  rights under siege in many republican  control legislators across the country  is set to pass in the  democratic-controlled house of  representatives but it's almost certain  to fail in the senate where it will not  pass the 60-vote threshold to break a  republican filibuster all the same the  push from politicians activists and even  actors is on including from the cw's new  initiative called freedom to vote  featuring their biggest stars i talked  to one of those stars elizabeth tullock  about her and her fellow actors efforts  to protect voter rights  betsy thanks so much for joining me on  the show this evening why did you decide  to take part in this psa video from the  cw network why is it so important to you  to speak out about voting rights right  now  i would say that uh democracy is sort of  under attack right now because there has  been a slew of voter suppression laws  that are either being introduced as  bills or straight up being past and  state legislatures all over the country  and there's nothing more important than  protecting  voting rights and ensuring that  everybody can participate participate  freely and also have the right to  fair access to cast those votes safely  and easily so i find what's going on  pretty frightening um this the cw's  freedom to  vote campaign is nonpartisan  and  i guess earlier this month on the 56th  anniversary of the john lewis passage of  the voting rights act they decided to to  start this campaign to encourage  congress to protect voting rights  yeah it's weird we live in a country  where suddenly protecting democracy  small d democracy is a partisan issue  that should be something we all agree on  wherever we are on the political  spectrum but as you say it's frightening  what's going on right now i wonder  though what i'm glad you guys are  speaking out  what about the argument from some on the  right that celebrities actors sports  stars should stay out of politics keep  their views to themselves or as fox's  laura ingram famously said in response  to lebron james shut up and  dribble would say that uh  the right to disagree is fundamentally  an american right and whether we  disagree about reproductive freedom or  climate change my voice is just as  important that it be heard as anybody  else's  and um i i personally feel that if you  have a platform to do  to to do good like do it and use it if  you feel comfortable with doing that and  again this is a nonpartisan campaign  we're all we're saying the message is  really simple we all deserve the right  to vote and everybody's voice should be  heard equally  yes well said just on voting rights in  american democracy you mentioned the the  john lewis voting rights act you met the  late congressman john lewis i believe  out of all places san diego comic con in  2016. uh there's a picture of you here  with him and your fellow actor russell  hornsby what was that like and what do  you make of the john lewis voting rights  act currently stuck in congress like  before the people act too  uh that was really uh  honestly overwhelming i was out to  dinner at this comic con in 2016. he was  there promoting his graphic novel march  and i saw him walk into the restaurant  and i almost started shaking because  he's such a civil rights icon and i i  talked to my co-star russell and i said  i'm never going to forgive myself if i  don't introduce myself and just thank  him for everything he's done for this  country and so he went up to him and  took a picture he's incredibly gracious  um i think it's very important  it is critical that the john lewis  voting rights advancement act is passed  um  because you know what happened in 2013  with the supreme court gunning certain  provisions like that of pre-clearance  all of that needs to be reinstated  pre-clearance is particularly important  because it ensured that jurisdictions  that had a history of discriminating  discriminating against voters of color  were required to have any changes to  their voting rules approved by the  federal government so we need federal  legislation to protect everyone um and  again what what we're already starting  to see since the 2020 election is  frightening and i read some statistics  that since june 2021 i think 17 states  have actually enacted laws with  restrictive provisions and so it is  really important to have some federal  oversight  definitely uh let's see what congress is  gonna do we've been uh covering it  heavily on this show we can only wait  and see and see if it ever gets through  the senate and this filibuster's ever  dealt with let me ask you this bitsy  have you always been a political person  interested in politics or did the trump  era radicalize you like it radicalized  so many otherwise previously a political  people in hollywood  it's a really good question i would i've  certainly never been apolitical um i've  always been somebody who wanted to use  my voice for good when i graduated from  college one of my very first jobs was  working as an assistant and researcher  for patrick cadell who was a democratic  pollster  um and so that was getting thrown into  fire we worked on um a bunch of stuff  together and uh but 100 i would say what  started happening in 2015 and 2016 with  trump um  ascension into power  absolutely that galvanized me more to  get even more political and to use my  platform in a way that can sometimes  feel scary because i come under a lot of  fire as i'm sure do other celebrities  who are using their voice and in ways  that not all of their fans agree with  and they get angry but um i don't know  that i could really look myself in the  face if i had an opportunity to speak up  and it didn't  so you mentioned your fans you mentioned  being an actor you're currently on the  cw playing one of the most iconic  on-screen journalists ever lois lane  giving some phenomenal performances  following in the footsteps of you know  margo donna terry hatcher what is it  like to play lois lane this legendary  journalist and what do you think some of  us real life journalists could learn  from her  just to uh  the thing that's the coolest thing about  her is just how entirely uncompromising  and unafraid she was abusing her voice  and  um i was originally cast in 2018 and  just seeing how  you know with this whole fake news and  the media is the enemy and everything  that's been popping up in the last few  years just the importance of  uh staying committed to  to truth and staying committed to um  [Music]  you know  just being truthful and  lois lane embodies this  more than any other  character and she is probably you know  one of us not the most famous  journalists on that we've seen on screen  and so it's been a  very humbling to be playing her at a  time when the profession of journalism  has come under siege especially in the  last few years  hi i'm mehdi hussain thanks for checking  out our channel on youtube you can see  more of the mehdi hassan show by  clicking on any of the videos on this  screen and make sure you subscribe below  to stay up to date on the day's biggest  stories thank you for watching

